{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n59 \n \n \nAs can be seen in Figure 1, the flame is nominally spherical, but heat loss to the burner tube causes it to \nlocally quench. Similarly, candle flames have an open gap close to the candle, again due to heat loss. These \nnon-premixed flames are a bit like bubbles, where the flame is the shell where the fuel and oxygen \ncombine and create heat, light, and chemical products like carbon dioxide and water. In premixed flames, \nthe fuel is within the \u201cbubble\u201d and the oxygen is outside of it. Future Flame Design tests are planned \nwhere the flame will be inside out, i.e., inverted. To conduct those tests (planned for 2020), the CIR's \nCombustion Chamber will be filled with a fuel/nitrogen mixture while an oxygen/nitrogen mixture will \nflow from the burner. \n \nWeek 9: May 3-9 \nOn GMT 123, 19 spherical flames were successfully ignited for ACME Flame Design. Gaseous ethylene \nfuel, typically diluted with nitrogen, was ejected into air from the porous sphere at the end of the \nburner. Compared to the test points on GMT 115, these tests were conducted with higher flow rates, \nwhich resulted in larger flames of shorter durations. \n \nIn most of the tests, the harp-like array of 14-micron ceramic fibers was translated into the flame to \ndetermine the radial temperature distribution. Where a fiber is visibly glowing, the temperature can be \ndetermined from its color, where yellow is hotter than orange, which in turn is hotter than red. The \nACME color camera was carefully characterized prior to launch enabling a quantitative determination of \nthe temperature by comparison of the red, green, and blue color planes in a measurement technique \nreferred to as pyrometry.  \n \nUnfortunately, during the day\u2019s final test point, the igniter tip burned out and will need to be replaced \nbefore performing more test points.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 59}}